# Method for Gear Production and Deburring

This invention presents a technique for manufacturing and finishing gear teeth on workpieces (6), utilizing a system with two workpiece spindles (3, 3′), a hobbing cutter (12), and a deburring unit (10). The process involves automatic loading of workpieces (6) into the spindles, followed by gear cutting via the hobbing tool (12) and subsequent deburring operations.

**The process unfolds through these steps:**

**a.** A raw, unprocessed workpiece (6) is picked up at the first loading station (19) by the first spindle (3), which then moves it toward the hobbing cutter (12);

**b.** The hobbing operation is performed;

**c.** The now-toothed workpiece (6) is set down at the first loading station (19), while simultaneously a new unmachined workpiece (6) is picked up;

**d.** The freshly-cut workpiece (6) is conveyed onward to the second loading station (19′);

**e.** At this second station (19′), the second spindle (3′) takes hold of the workpiece (6) and delivers it to the deburring station (10);

**f.** The deburring process is carried out on the workpiece (6).

A buffer zone (14) capable of holding at least one workpiece (6) is positioned between the two loading stations (19, 19′). Workpieces (6) pass through this intermediate storage area before reaching the second loading position (19′). 

Notably, the operations described in steps e. and f. occur concurrently with the hobbing operation in step b. — meaning the deburring cycle for one workpiece overlaps in time with the gear-cutting cycle of another, allowing the two processes to run in parallel rather than sequentially.

